    body_en: "As electric vehicles spread, what to do with batteries that have reached the end of their life has emerged as a new industrial challenge. Industry estimates project that EV batteries classified as waste batteries in Korea will rise to tens of thousands within the next five years. Even when no longer suitable for driving, EV batteries often retain residual capacity, drawing attention for potential reuse as energy storage devices. Accordingly, startups specializing in battery reuse and recycling have emerged one after another in recent years. Automakers and battery manufacturers are also entering this market by building collection systems for used batteries. Experts, however, cite the lack of a standardized method for accurately assessing residual battery performance as a limitation. Unclear performance evaluation inevitably causes confusion in the safety and pricing of reused products. There are also concerns that the recycling process for recovering rare metals such as lithium and cobalt remains costly, making economic viability difficult to secure. The government has said it will build an integrated management system for used batteries and expand support for related companies. Experts say it remains to be seen whether this market can achieve the two goals of resource circulation and new industry cultivation at the same time.",
